'' __FB_64BIT__ came from an '#ifdef' at tiko-lang's OWN compile time, so it was true even
'' for '-target linux-x86_64'. Getting it wrong does not fail -- it silently selects
'' the other half of every header, and the corpus branches on it 214 times.
''
'' WHAT THIS CAN STILL PROVE, and what it cannot. win32 was the only target on which
'' __FB_64BIT__ was ABSENT, so its removal (D14) takes with it the case that
'' distinguished a target-derived macro from a host-derived one THROUGH THAT MACRO.
'' Both remaining targets define it. The platform macros still discriminate, so this
'' now pins -target reaching the predefine table via __FB_LINUX__ from a Windows host,
'' which is the same mechanism and a weaker witness. Recorded rather than quietly
'' downgraded.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --pp-only -target linux-x86_64 tests/driver/src/predefines.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0
'' EXPECT-OUT: HAS64
'' EXPECT-OUT: LIN
'' EXPECT-NOT: WIN
